// Fixture: numeric_range_float_boundary_overflow
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §5 -- numeric range enforcement, float case, "applies
// to floats overflowing to infinity too." Merged fixture (per explicit resolution --
// float range and float overflow are the SAME phenomenon, not two distinct concepts:
// for a float, the only way to be out-of-range for a given width is for the magnitude
// to exceed what that width can finitely represent, which manifests exactly as the
// value becoming infinite when coerced to that width). A separate "float boundary"
// fixture and a separate "float overflow" fixture would test the identical mechanism
// twice under different names -- this single fixture covers both.
//
// The rejecting case is deliberately produced by COMPUTATION (multiplying a
// near-max-valid value further) rather than a hand-picked, suspiciously-round
// out-of-range literal -- demonstrating genuine overflow, not just "the compiler
// rejects big numbers on sight."

define Stats
	value = f32

Stats NearMaxValid
	value = 3.4e38
	// near f32's max finite magnitude (~3.4028235e38) but safely within range --
	// must be accepted.

Stats Overflow
	value = 3.4e38
	value * 10
	// multiplying a near-max-valid f32 value by 10 pushes its magnitude well past
	// what f32 can finitely represent -- the result is mathematically infinite once
	// coerced to f32's range. ERROR expected: rejected as overflow, NOT silently
	// stored as `inf`.

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   NearMaxValid.value == 3.4e38 (or the nearest exact f32 representation thereof)
//   Overflow: compile error against field `value` -- out of range for `f32`
//     (magnitude exceeds finite representable range, i.e. would become infinite).
//     Phase 6, check "numeric_range". If a reference implementation instead silently
//     stores `inf` (or some sentinel) here, that's exactly the failure mode this
//     fixture exists to catch -- the spec explicitly rules out silent
//     wrapping/clamping, and infinity-on-overflow is named as an explicit case of
//     that, not an exception to it.
